### Create a tenant & manage pending tenants (uc.admin.create-tenant, Slice A / cr-0009)
# Runnable acceptance for the Server-Owner tenant API. Run against any running instance — every path here
# is a pre-auth rejection or boundary validation, so no seed is needed.
#
# The authed happy path (create 201 → list → resend → reassign → revoke) lives in admin/tenant-authed.http
# (signed Server-Owner login via ../_lib/ed25519.js; fresh instance). The non-owner 403 still needs a
# *member* session, which no http file can seed yet (setup only mints the owner), so it stays covered by the
# backend slice (TenantsSliceTest) + the web round-trip (cr-0009 phase 4). This file covers what needs no
# signature against ANY instance: that every endpoint rejects an
# anonymous/bogus caller with a uniform 401. Since cr-0012 centralized auth behind @RolesAllowed (eager
# security), auth is enforced BEFORE both body validation and tenant lookup — so even a malformed body or a
# well-formed tenant id returns 401, never 400/404, and no validation detail leaks pre-auth. The boundary
# 400s (invalid domain/name/email) are now asserted with an owner token by TenantsSliceTest.
#
# The Slice B (cr-0010) accept family has mixed auth: `accept` is @Authenticated (anonymous → 401, like the
# admin endpoints), while `accept/preview` and `accept/register` are @PermitAll (gated only by the bootstrap
# secret), so they REACH the handler with no session — an unknown secret is 404 and a malformed body is 400.
# The signed happy path (a real bootstrap secret + a valid grant) is not http-expressible (the secret lives
# only in the invite email and the grant needs ML-KEM), so it is covered by TenantAcceptSliceTest + the web
# round-trip (cr-0010 phase 4).
#
# Run: ijhttp --env-file http/admin/http-client.env.json --env dev http/admin/tenant.http

### 1. Create with no bearer → generic 401 (no anonymous tenant creation)
POST {{host}}/api/tenants
Content-Type: application/json

{ "tenantName": "Familie", "domain": "family.example.com", "ownerEmail": "kevin@example.com" }

> {%
client.test("anonymous create is a generic 401", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected code authentication_failed");
});
%}

### 2. Create with a bogus bearer → the SAME generic 401
POST {{host}}/api/tenants
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer this-token-was-never-issued

{ "tenantName": "Familie", "domain": "family.example.com", "ownerEmail": "kevin@example.com" }

> {%
client.test("bogus-bearer create is a generic 401", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected code authentication_failed");
});
%}

### 3. Create with an invalid domain but no bearer → 401 (auth precedes validation, no pre-auth leak)
POST {{host}}/api/tenants
Content-Type: application/json

{ "tenantName": "Familie", "domain": "NOT a domain", "ownerEmail": "kevin@example.com" }

> {%
client.test("anonymous create with invalid domain is still a generic 401", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected code authentication_failed");
});
%}

### 4. Create with a blank tenant name but no bearer → 401 (auth precedes validation)
POST {{host}}/api/tenants
Content-Type: application/json

